# `'\xNN'` -- exactly two hex digits, either case, in a CHAR literal only.
#
# Fixed width on purpose. C's `\x` is greedy over hex digits, and that greed is
# the reason `\xNN` stays out of STRING literals: a string literal's text is
# carried as ESCAPED source all the way to codegen, and the adjacent-literal
# join (docs/spec/01-lexical.md §3.9.4) concatenates that escaped text, so
# `"\x4" "1"` would join into `\x41` and mean one byte where the author wrote
# two. tests/reject/hex_escape_in_string.ty pins that refusal. A char literal
# has no such exposure -- it is decoded to a byte at lex time (src/tychoc.c:498)
# and never reaches a C string literal -- which is also why `\0` was already
# legal here and not there.
fn main():
    println(str('\x41' == 'A') + "," + str('\x61' == 'a') + "," + str('\x30' == '0'))

    # either case of hex digit, and the escapes it overlaps agree
    println(str('\x0a' == '\n') + "," + str('\x0A' == '\n') + "," + str('\x09' == '\t'))

    # the bytes that have no other spelling: NUL, ESC, and the top of the range
    println(str(to_int('\x00')) + "," + str(to_int('\x1b')) + "," + str(to_int('\xFF')))

    # and it is an ordinary char: arithmetic, comparison, appending
    e := '\x1b'
    println(str(to_int(e + 1)) + "," + str(e < '\x20') + "," + str(len("" + e)))
